# Figure & Caption

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A figure holds a picture, diagram, chart, code sample or quotation that makes sense on its own and that the text refers to. Its caption says why it is there or where it comes from. This page owns the figure and its caption. For when to use an image and how to write its alt text, see [Images](images.html). For a credited quotation, see [Blockquote](blockquote.html).

This page covers:

- Figure & Caption
  - `<figure>`
    - `<figcaption>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- The figure wraps the item and its caption.
- The item: an image, a diagram, a chart or a quotation.
- The caption, as the first or last child of the figure.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-figure"><code>&lt;figure&gt;</code></h3>

A standard HTML element for self-contained content. It may hold one caption, as its first or last child. A browser treats it as a figure and, where the caption is present, can use the caption as its name. You can also name it with `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby`. Do not use a figure as a general wrapper.

Home Assistant has no figure element for dashboard authors to add. Use a figure on Civica pages and in custom cards you write. In a Markdown card, put a plain paragraph under the image that says what it shows or why it is there; for quotations in a Markdown card, see [Blockquote](blockquote.html).

Browsers give a figure a margin of 1em above and below and 40 pixels each side. Set the margin yourself when the image should fill the width.

```html
<figure>
  <img src="floor-plan.svg" alt="Floor plan: the living room is to the right of the hall and kitchen.">
  <figcaption>Invented plan, not to scale. The blue dot is the living-room sensor.</figcaption>
</figure>
```

<h3 id="child-figure--figcaption"><code>&lt;figcaption&gt;</code></h3>

The caption. It has no role of its own; it gives the figure its name in current browsers, though support varies. It is not a replacement for alt text: the alt text describes the image, and the caption says why it matters or where it comes from. Do not repeat one in the other.

The Civica theme sets no variable for these two elements. Caption text can use `secondary-text-color` (#484949 in light mode, #CBD0D3 in dark mode), borders use `divider-color` (#CECECE in light mode, #50565A in dark mode) and an image backdrop can use `secondary-background-color` (#F3F2F1 in light mode, #30363A in dark mode).

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A caption sits next to its image, so people do not hunt for the explanation.
- Not every screen reader announces the caption as the figure’s name.
- A figure is not something a dashboard author can add as a card. Use a Markdown card when you need an image with text beneath it.
- A figure with a long caption can crowd a narrow card. Keep the caption to a sentence or two.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a figure when the item needs a caption. For a decorative picture, use no figure and an empty alt attribute. Write the caption for the reader, not for the picture: say what to look at and why.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content: the image has alt text, and the caption adds to it.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the caption belongs to its figure in the markup.
- 1.4.5 Images of Text: do not put the caption inside the picture.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: the image scales and the caption wraps at 320 pixels.
- 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum): check the caption on your own theme before you claim a ratio.

A caption beside the image, with one clear point, keeps the load low.

<h3 id="check">Check it works</h3>

Check the image with the caption read alone and with the alt text read alone. Test at 320 pixels and in both light and dark modes. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

